thanks for the fix @anencore94 ! I'm interested in this issue too. 

In the non-Hamony path, should the newly added dict-form branches for `type =="reasoning"` and `type =="assistant"` follow the same merge policy as the model-form branches at `ResponseReasoningItem` [utils.py#L203-L211](https://github.com/anencore94/vllm/blob/90207d057eaf47f67ce949a221a428559a8071ed/vllm/entrypoints/openai/responses/utils.py#L203-L211) and `ResponseOutputMessage` [utils.py#L217-L225](https://github.com/anencore94/vllm/blob/90207d057eaf47f67ce949a221a428559a8071ed/vllm/entrypoints/openai/responses/utils.py#L217-L225)?

In the model-form path, reasoning item followed by assistant output is rendered as **one assistant turn**. The new dict-form path always return a fresh assistant message, so the same logical history can render as **two consecutive assistant** turns. 

I reproduced this with:
```
# Serve model
CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-8B \
  --port 8000 \
  --served-model-name test_model

# Call model-form
curl -sS http://localhost:8000/v1/responses \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "test_model",
    "enable_response_messages": true,
    "max_output_tokens": 1,
    "input": [
      {
        "type": "reasoning",
        "id": "rs_1",
        "status": "completed",
        "summary": [],
        "content": [
          {"type": "reasoning_text", "text": "REASONING_SENTINEL"}
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "message",
        "role": "assistant",
        "id": "msg_1",
        "status": "completed",
        "content": [
          {"type": "output_text", "text": "ANSWER_SENTINEL", "annotations": []}
        ]
      },
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "Reply OK."
      }
    ]
  }' | jq -r '.input_messages[0].message' > ./target/model_prompt.txt

# Call dict-form
curl -sS http://localhost:8000/v1/responses \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "test_model",
    "enable_response_messages": true,
    "max_output_tokens": 1,
    "input": [
      {
        "type": "reasoning",
        "id": "rs_1",
        "status": "completed",
        "summary": [],
        "content": [
          {"type": "reasoning_text", "text": "REASONING_SENTINEL"}
        ]
      },
      {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "ANSWER_SENTINEL"
      },
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "Reply OK."
      }
    ]
  }' | jq -r '.input_messages[0].message' > ./target/dict_prompt.txt
```
The dict-form has one more empty  **assistant** turn.

@TheDuyIT good catch, and apologies for the very slow reply — you were right. The dict-form `type == "reasoning"` and `role == "assistant"` branches should follow the same previous-assistant-message merge policy as the model-form branches; otherwise a dict-form reasoning item followed by an assistant message renders as two consecutive assistant turns instead of one merged turn. That's now aligned in the latest push: both dict-form branches merge into the preceding assistant message under the same contract as their model-form counterparts.

Also rebased onto the latest `main`. The branch had fallen a long way behind, so it's squashed into a single commit to keep the review readable. Two upstream behaviours I deliberately preserved while resolving the conflicts, for transparency:

- #44361's guard that string-content assistant messages stay `EasyInputMessageParam` dicts rather than being coerced, so no `id`/`status` get injected into those.
- Upstream's choice to append the original, un-normalized item on the `ValidationError` fallback path (this PR previously appended the normalized dict there) — the newer behaviour gives better Pydantic error reporting.

@chaunceyjiang this should also settle your earlier question: the PR still routes everything through `input_item_parsing`, it just extracts the per-item defaulting into a helper rather than adding a parallel path.

I'll re-push shortly with the sign-off trailer restored on the squashed commit (DCO is red purely because of that, not the content). PTAL when either of you has a chance — this was blocking Responses-API adoption for at least one other team in this thread.

